lessons.bodyMarkdown:
# Resources

A **Resource** in Godot is a data container that can be saved to `.tres` files and inspected in the editor. For Kadi we will model each card's static data — its name, art path, and rule text — as a Resource.

```gdscript
class_name CardData
extends Resource

@export var suit: CardTypes.Suits
@export var rank: int
@export var display_name: String
@export var art: Texture2D
@export var rule_text: String = ""

func is_penalty() -> bool:
    return rank == 2 or rank == 3 or rank == 15
```

`@export` exposes fields to the Inspector, so designers can build decks by editing `.tres` files in the editor — no code, no recompiling. This is the whole point of Resources: data and code separate cleanly.

Resources are reference-counted and cheap to share. Two players can hold the same CardData reference without copying.

> [!WHY]
> Resources move card definitions out of code and into editable files. A designer can rebalance a penalty value, swap art, or rename a card without touching GDScript. You ship the same binary with different `.tres` packs for, say, a kids' variant — pure data, zero risk to logic.

Prefer Resources over dictionaries for any data that a non-programmer will touch.

